      "original_context": {
        "full_target": "Here dimension means **Hausdorff dimension**. As approved by the user on\n2026-10-23, assume explicitly that \\(E\\) is nonempty. With the convention\n\\(\\dim_H\\varnothing=0\\), the printed equality would otherwise fail for\nthe empty set. This is a disclosed addition to the printed exercise.\n\nLet \\(n\\geq0\\) be an integer, let \\(E\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) be a nonempty\ncompact set, and put \\(I=[0,1]\\). Identify \\(\\mathbb R^n\\times\\mathbb R\\)\nwith \\(\\mathbb R^{n+1}\\) and use its Euclidean metric. Prove\n\n\\[\n\\dim_H(E\\times[0,1])=\\dim_H E+1.\n\\]\n\nThe zero-dimensional ambient space \\(\\mathbb R^0\\) is included. A later\nformalization using a different product norm must justify the metric change.",
        "hypotheses": [
          {
            "id": "H001",
            "statement": "E is nonempty.",
            "origin": "problem.md:16–23; approved addition stated at lines 16–19."
          },
          {
            "id": "H002",
            "statement": "E is compact in Euclidean ℝ^n₀.",
            "origin": "problem.md:21–23."
          }
        ],
        "conclusion": "dimH(G)=dimH(E)+1, where E has its original Euclidean metric and G is E×[0,1] identified with Euclidean Rn(n₀+1) as specified in the selected target; this holds for every natural n₀, including n₀=0. Both sides are finite, so the equality also holds for real Hausdorff dimensions."
      },
